The hardest sale happens inside the fab

Suppose a foundry is preparing a new gate-all-around transistor process. Several suppliers offer machines capable of handling one difficult step. One has the fastest advertised throughput. Another has better laboratory precision. A third already works reliably with the foundry's neighboring machines.

Which wins?

We learned the physical jobs of lithography, deposition and etch in Lessons 005–007. The new question is how a fab chooses *whose* machine to install. Unlike buying an interchangeable commodity, adopting a process tool can change the factory's chemistry, defect rates, production schedule and final chip performance. That creates a very different competitive market.

What the customer actually qualifies

A supplier first demonstrates a process on test wafers. Engineers check whether its output meets a narrow specification—for example, removing a temporary material while preserving the transistor channel immediately beside it.

But a successful demonstration is only the beginning. The process must keep working across thousands of wafers, different chambers, maintenance cycles and small variations in incoming material. Engineers call the allowable range of conditions the **process window**. A tool that works only at one perfectly tuned setting may be less valuable than a slightly slower tool that works reliably across normal production variation.

Qualification also asks whether the tool damages a later step. An invisible surface change can disrupt the next deposited film. A tiny increase in defective chips may outweigh a saving in processing time. The customer's real output is good chips, not completed tool cycles.

Three suppliers, three distinct bets

Consider three current approaches to the transition from older FinFET transistors to gate-all-around designs, which put several thin conducting channels under one surrounding gate.

**Lam Research — selective removal.** In one critical operation, a manufacturer must remove a temporary silicon-germanium layer from between silicon channels without eating away the channels. Lam markets selective-etch tools designed to remove material laterally and with high precision. This is not simply faster drilling: the value is removing one material while leaving its neighbor intact.

**ASM International — selective growth.** Its epitaxy equipment grows carefully controlled crystalline semiconductor material in specified locations, including regions that supply current to a transistor. Growing the right material with the right crystalline quality is a different challenge from removing it. The supplier's expertise lies in growth chemistry, surface preparation and uniformity.

